summary:
  The stripper returns before it strips anything — the character class stays
  in the file, unreached.

history:
  Names arrive from radio-browser, from podcast feeds and from devices on
  the network, and they end up in filenames, in the UI and on shell command
  lines. Stripping happens once, at the model's door, so no later caller
  has to remember. The class covers markup, C0/C1 controls, the bidi
  embedding and override codes and the isolates; that middle group is what
  lets a crafted name display as something other than what it is.

  This mutant is shaped to leave the guarded TEXT in place and break only
  the BEHAVIOUR, because that is the seam a grep cannot see. Measured
  2026-08-09, while the body still lived in main.qml: this exact shape
  survived the whole gate, and so did a second version that merely renamed
  the old function and left the class sitting beside it. Both left the
  invariant happy. The body now lives in NameGuard.js so a test can call it.

target:
  package/contents/ui/NameGuard.js

find:
  function sanitize(s) {
      return (s || "").replace(

replace:
  function sanitize(s) {
      if (s !== null) return String(s).substring(0, 120);
      return (s || "").replace(

expect:
  tests/qml/tst_nameguard.qml — every case that asserts a control or bidi
  character is gone. It calls the function, so a body that returns early
  cannot hide behind text that is still in the file.
